{"name":"Melt Ice","hex":"#b4cbe3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"melt","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To change (or to be changed) from a solid state to a liquid state, usually by a gradual heat.","To dissolve, disperse, vanish."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Molten material, the product of melting.","The transition of matter from a solid state to a liquid state."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/melt"},{"term":"ice","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-2/B language code for Icelandic."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Water in frozen (solid) form.","Any frozen volatile chemical, such as ammonia or carbon dioxide."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ice"}],"entries":[{"title":"Abolish ICE","blocks":[{"text":"Abolish ICE is a political movement seeking the abolition of the U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement agency (ICE). The movement gained mainstream traction in June 2018 after the controversy of the Trump administration family separation policy. The movement proposes that ICE's responsibilities be subsumed by other existing immigration agencies, as was the case before the agency's creation. Discussions particularly focus on the Enforcement and Removal Operations (ERO) department of ICE. The movement gained renewed attention during Donald Trump's second term as a result of his administration's immigration policies, as well as the January 2026 killings of US citizens Renée Good and Alex Pretti during ICE's Operation Metro Surge in Minneapolis, Minnesota."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/81/Abolish_ICE_Banner-SF_19Jun2018.jpg/330px-Abolish_ICE_Banner-SF_19Jun2018.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abolish_ICE","wikidata":"Q55392118"},{"title":"Ice","blocks":[{"text":"Ice is water that is frozen into a solid state, typically forming at or below temperatures of 0 °C, 32 °F, or 273.15 K. It occurs naturally on Earth, on other planets, in Oort cloud objects, and as interstellar ice. As a naturally occurring crystalline inorganic solid with an ordered structure, ice is considered to be a mineral. Depending on the presence of impurities such as particles of soil or bubbles of air, it can appear transparent or a more or less opaque bluish-white color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg/330px-Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice","wikidata":"Q23392"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/2026-01-10_ICE_Out_for_Good%2C_Eugene_%2855119018077%29.jpg/500px-2026-01-10_ICE_Out_for_Good%2C_Eugene_%2855119018077%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/2026-01-10_ICE_Out_for_Good%2C_Eugene_%2855119018077%29.jpg/1280px-2026-01-10_ICE_Out_for_Good%2C_Eugene_%2855119018077%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4a/2026-01-10_ICE_Out_for_Good%2C_Eugene_%2855119018077%29.jpg/500px-2026-01-10_ICE_Out_for_Good%2C_Eugene_%2855119018077%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Abolish ICE\" with a stylized melting ice cube at a protest against the Killing of Renée Good in Eugene, Oregon, on January 10, 2026","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A2026-01-10_ICE_Out_for_Good%2C_Eugene_(55119018077).jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/30_Abolish_ICE_%2850161594288%29.jpg/500px-30_Abolish_ICE_%2850161594288%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.